expr main [(two a breast), (too abreast)]

let message "Did you mean `two abreast`?"
let description "Corrects wrong variants of `two abreast`."
let kind "Spelling"
let becomes "two abreast"

test "When they're two a breast it often makes it unsafe to pass when it would have been safe one a breast." "When they're two abreast it often makes it unsafe to pass when it would have been safe one a breast."
test "Otherwise it's too abreast with one-handed weapons and one person with a two-handed swinging weapon." "Otherwise it's two abreast with one-handed weapons and one person with a two-handed swinging weapon."
test "Two a breast should only be done on quiet roads, or at off peak hours." "Two abreast should only be done on quiet roads, or at off peak hours."
test "They ride two a breast because they've been led to believe it's perfectly fine and the thing to do" "They ride two abreast because they've been led to believe it's perfectly fine and the thing to do"
test "They are still too abreast. There's a potential for them to stay side by side all lap you know but it's not going to happen." "They are still two abreast. There's a potential for them to stay side by side all lap you know but it's not going to happen."
test "Cyclists move fast and likely unaware of the driveways and possibly are riding too abreast." "Cyclists move fast and likely unaware of the driveways and possibly are riding two abreast."

